* Opening preempts

Booklet section "OPENING PREEMPTS": a 3- or 4-level opening tends toward sound at
equal or unfavorable vulnerability (within two or three tricks of the bid) and
lighter at favorable vulnerability (within four tricks). The booklet gives no
numeric HCP/length table, so the ranges below (7 cards for a 3-level preempt, 8+
for a 4-level preempt, all in the 5--10/11 HCP band) are an interpolation from the
"rule of 2/3/4" and standard practice; see NOTES.md #5. Vulnerability itself is not
part of the description vocabulary, so the trick-count discipline is left to the
sampler/user rather than encoded here.

3C = {prio:51} 7=!c, 5--9 hcp
3D = {prio:52} 7=!d, 5--9 hcp
3H = {prio:53} 7=!h, 5--9 hcp
3S = {prio:54} 7=!s, 5--9 hcp
4C = {prio:55} 8+!c, 5--10 hcp
4D = {prio:56} 8+!d, 5--10 hcp
4H = {prio:57} 8+!h, 5--10 hcp
4S = {prio:58} 8+!s, 5--10 hcp

** Responses to a 3-level preempt

A raise is to play (further preemption); a new suit is forcing; 3NT is to play;
4NT is Blackwood.

3C-
4C = unlimited
3D = F
3H = F
3S = F
3N = unlimited
4N = !BW

3D-
4D = unlimited
3H = F
3S = F
3N = unlimited
4N = !BW

3H-
4H = unlimited
3S = F
3N = unlimited
4N = !BW

3S-
4S = unlimited
3N = unlimited
4N = !BW

** Responses to a 4-level preempt

At the 4-level the only realistic continuations are to pass, raise further, or
ask for aces.

4C-
5C = unlimited
4N = !BW

4D-
5D = unlimited
4N = !BW

4H-
4N = !BW

4S-
4N = !BW
